--TEST--
a granted root reads while a path outside it is refused, including one that reaches outside through `..` — `rule:security/capability-question-is-grant-and-scope`, ADR 0104 § 3
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Inside the granted root: this is the whole of what the grant bought.
echo Core\IO::read('./data/note.txt');

// Outside it, and the file exists — so a refusal here is the capability's and
// not the filesystem's. `nvs_runtime::capability::open_read`'s own doc owns
// the ordering that makes that true: the check runs before the open, so a
// path outside the grant is refused whether or not it exists and the two
// messages cannot be used to probe a directory.
try {
    echo Core\IO::read('./secret.txt');
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}

// The same file reached through a `..` segment out of the granted root. A
// comparison done on the literal path would accept this one, which is why the
// grant is canonicalised before it is compared.
try {
    echo Core\IO::read('./data/../secret.txt');
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["./data"]
--FILE data/note.txt--
note
--FILE secret.txt--
secret
--EXPECT--
note
Core\IO::read needs the capability `fs.read` for ./secret.txt, which is not granted
Core\IO::read needs the capability `fs.read` for ./data/../secret.txt, which is not granted
